    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Honda Racing Releases Its First-Ever In-House Made Parts for the Civic Type R and Integra Type S\nAnother Big Airline Turns to Domestically-Produced Sustainable Fuel\n7 photos\nPhoto: Singapore Airlines\nSingapore Airlines (SIA) has officially agreed to purchase a considerable quantity of SAF (sustainable aviation fuel) from Aether, a startup with offices in the United States and South East Asia. The SAF will be produced in Singapore, at Aether’s brand-new Project Beacon.\nSIA is now officially the first airline to purchase SAF from Project Beacon, a new commercial production facility that is still under development at a refining complex on Pulau Bukom, Singapore. Aether was incubated just a few years ago, in 2022, by Xora, a deep tech venture firm backed by Temasek.\nThe startup, which describes itself as a climate technology company, operates a research and development hub in Chicago, as well as an extended team in Singapore. At the beginning of last year, when Aether and SIA first signed a MoU (memorandum of understanding) regarding a regular SAF supply, Aether hadn’t even decided upon the location of its first production facility.\nNow, when the final purchase agreement became official, Aether is already working on Project Beacon, the facility that will make SAF right there in Singapore. There are many types of SAF, and many ways to produce it. Aether’s proprietary Aurora technology claims to increase production efficiency while reducing capital cost.\nSAF will be obtained from feedstock, but instead of the conventional HEFA (hydro-processed esters and fatty acids), it will rely on the Fischer-Tropsch process that converts gases derived from waste into liquid fuel. Aether claims that its proprietary technology results in “near-ideal” carbon conversion efficiency, while also using a more diverse range of feedstocks.\nProject Beacon is only the beginning. Aether plans to being operating its first large-scale commercial facility, with a capacity ten times greater than Beacon right now, by 2031. The SAF that will be produced at Beacon is certified under the Carbon Offsetting and Reduction Scheme for International Aviation (CORSIA) and will be blended with conventional jet fuel according to current regulations, before being supplied to several airports served by SIA.\nAccording to the final agreement, SIA will purchase up to 4,800 tons of SAF from Aether, over the course of three years, with first deliveries starting in 2028.\nMore and more major airlines and different operators are trying to secure consistent supplies of SAF, preferably from domestic sources. SAF is still considered the fastest, most reliable way of effectively reducing airplane CO2 emissions right now.\nIn the US, American Airlines marked one of the biggest milestones in the industry this year, with the first passenger flight powered not by SAF, but eSAF, which is the even greener version of SAF. eSAF is obtained using waste carbon and sustainable electricity.\nThe American Airlines eSAF flight took off from Corpus Christi International Airport (CRP) and landed at Dallas Fort Worth International Airport (DFW), powered by eSAF produced locally in Texas by Infinium.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nSAF Singapore Airlines Aether sustainable aviation fuel\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT Original Owner, Sons Reunite With 1962 Pontiac Catalina 421 SD He Had New, They Last Saw It in 1972",
